Materials and methods Materials Analytical grade reagents and chemicals were used in this study. Chemical magnesium nitrate (99% purity), Zinc acetate dehydrate (98% purity), and NaOH (98%) were purchased from Spectrum Reagents and Chemicals Pvt. Ltd. (India), respectively. Pure Chia seeds and Rosemary leaves were purchased from a branded online store, Neutra Veda. Fresh Neem leaves were collected from the campus of Madan Mohan Malaviya University of Technology. The Rosemary leaves were about 100% pure as per supplier’s data. Solutions were prepared using distilled water. Their detailed discussion of the methodology is as follows. Synthesis of MgO and ZnO NPs The preparation of NPs using plant extracts has several advantages. This reduced the use of reactive chemicals. Chia (Salvia hispanica L.) belongs to the Lamiaceae family of the plant kingdom, with characteristics of an annual herbaceous plant. Chia is well known as a source of oil, protein, dietary fibre, minerals, and polyphenolic compounds with medicinal value, which is consumed as oilseed, flour, whole seeds, and mucilage. The oil content of the Chia seeds with enhanced polyunsaturated omega-3-linolenic acid is the major cause for the increasing interest of the consumers. In addition, Chia seeds are also known for their richness in natural antioxidants such as tocopherols, phytosterols, carotenoids, and phenolic compounds, including chlorogenic acid, caffeic acid, myricetin, quercetin, and kaempferol, which lead to benefits on human health 3 . The presence of these phytochemicals make them biocompatible and useful for in vitro applications because our body does not react against their presence in form of traces left during synthesis if any. After washing Chia seeds multiple times with distilled water and allowing them to dry, we took 3 g of Chiaseeds (Salvia hispanica L.) in 100 mL of distilled water (DW). The mixture was then stirred at 60 °C for 2 h. The aqueous extract was then filtered Fig.  2 . Before synthesizing MgO-NPs, solution A (0.3 M Mg (NO3)2.6H2O) (100 mL) was prepared. Solution A was continuously stirred, and 10 mL of aqueous Salvia hispanica L. (Chia) extract was gradually added drop wise. The resulting mixture was stirred for 18 and 1/2 hours at 70 °C because temperature offers the best balance between the reaction kinetics and precursor stability. 70 °C was chosen as the stirring temperature during MgO synthesis. In addition to facilitate homogeneous nucleation and the development of MgO particles, 70 °C improves reactant solubility and diffusion, reducing fast component evaporation or degradation. It has been demonstrated that well-dispersed and uniform nanoparticles can be produced under these conditions 40 . Yellow residue in the beaker was filtered and calcined at 300 °C for 4 h, yielding the final white MgO nanopowder. The preparation of MgO NPs by the sol gel method is illustrated in Fig.  3 . 
 Fig. 2 Method for the preparation of Chia seed extract. 

 Fig. 3 Preparation of MgO Nanoparticles by sol-gel methods. 
 A schematic representation for preparing the extract of leaves of Dahlia Pinnata plant is shown in Fig.  4 . We chose the leaves of Dahlia Pinnata as they contain bioactive organic molecules and phytochemicals that may reduce the metal ions and stabilize the colloidal particles in the reacting solution 41 , 42  Dahlia leaves were washed two or three times with distilled water. After letting the leaves dry for three hours at room temperature, they were mixed with 100 mL of deionised water to make a paste. The paste was spun at 800 rpm. Room temperature, or approximately 30 °C, was used to create the yellow-colored extract. The extract was filtered using a Whatman filter paper. Eventually, the color of the extract turned reddish brown. 
 Fig. 4 Schematic representation of preparing Dahlia pinnata extract. 
 We uniformly dissolved 3.3 g of zinc acetate dihydrate (ZnC4H6O4) in 50 mL of distilled water in a beaker and agitated it at 400 rpm for 30 min at 40 °C using a hot-plate magnetic stirrer. The solution was stirred for one hour at 80 °C and 500 rpm, and then 20 mL of yellow-colored leaf extract was added drop-wise at a rate of 20 drops per minute. We then gradually added sodium hydroxide (NaOH) to maintain appropriate pH values throughout the process. The white precipitate at pH 10 was oven-dried, mortar-ground into a fine white powder, and calcined for four hours at 300 °C in a muffle furnace. Figure  5  shows the preparation of ZnO NPs using the sol-gel method. 42 
 Fig. 5 Schematic representation of different steps involved in preparation of ZnO Nanoparticles. 
 Preparation of CQDs from Neem and Rosemary extracts After gathering fresh Neem leaves, they wsere thoroughly washed in both taps and then distilled water 2–3 times. After being baked dry at 40 °C in an oven for 24 h, it was crushed into powder. 200 mL of distilled water and 12 g of powdered Neem leaves were mixed well using a magnetic stirrer at 30 °C and 400 rpm for one hour. The resulting extract was centrifuged at 4000 rpm. In Fig.  6 , the CDs were created by a straightforward thermal evaporation method using Azadirachta indica (Neem) extract as the carbon source. We took 200 mL of plant extract into a 250 mL flask and used an oven to irradiate it at 150 °C for six hours. We purposefully used the reaction time of 6 h at 150 °C to improve the process energy efficiency and prevent undue heat deterioration, based on our earlier optimization trials. We used a unique but straightforward method to further improve the synthesis conditions and avoid charring by placing a second beaker filled with distilled water next to the beaker containing the Neem and Rosemary extracts separately inside a hot air oven. This setup is intended to create a saturated hot air microenvironment that effectively balanced the ambient humidity and minimized the thermal stress leading to abrupt charring process. The gentle release of water vapor from the neighboring beaker maintained a controlled atmosphere throughout the evaporation process, ensuring smooth carbonization without any charring. This innovative, cheap and accessible method may provide better reproducibility. The greener processing conditions make the synthesis process effective and environmentally friendly 19 . Irradiation was performed carefully using a hot air oven to prevent spillage. After the product had cooled to ambient temperature, it was centrifuged for 15 min at 4000 rpm to remove larger-sized agglomerated particles. The supernatant containing CDs was collected using a syringe filter. CDs suspended in water prepared using Neem extract were stored at 4 °C. 
 Fig. 6 Preparation of CDs from Neem extract. 
 Dried Rosemary leaves were ground into a coarse powder. Further, 8 g of Rosemary leaves was mixed in 200 mL of distilled water at 30 °C and stirred for one hour at 400 rpm. Rosemary extract (200 mL) was placed in a 250 mL flask and irradiated at 150 °C for six hours in a hot air oven. Irradiation was performed carefully to avoid spills. After allowing the mixture to cool to ambient temperature, the solution was centrifuged for 15 min at 4000 rpm. The supernatant containing the CDs was then collected using a syringe filter. Rosemary extracts CDs suspended in water were kept at 4 °C for storage. Figure  7  shows the preparation of Rosemary extracts CDs. 
 Fig. 7 Schematic flow-chart for preparation of CDs using Rosemary extract. 
 Coating of nanoparticles on microtiter plates Figure  8  (a) shows the coating scheme of 2,5-Dimercapto-1,3,4-thiadiazole on polystyrene microtiter plates. We mixed 100 mg of 2,5-Dimercapto-1,3,4-thiadiazole in 100 mL methanol and stirred for 20 min at 400 rpm. Then, we dropped the solution into the wells of microtiter plates and allowed the solvent to evaporate by maintaining a temperature of 60 °C in an oven for 24 h. We coated the functionalized plate with 2, 4, 6, 8, 12, and 16 mg of MgO and ZnO nanoparticles by dissolving them in 25 mL of distilled water. The solutions of ZnO and MgO NPs prepared in distilled water were evaporated by keeping the microtiter plate at 60 °C in an oven for 24 h. The concentrations C1, C2, C3, C4, and C5 were 2, 4, 6, 8, and 16 mg per 25 mL DW, respectively. The heating process not only allows the use of 2,5-Dimercapto-1,3,4-thiadiazole on the microtiter plate, but CDs and metal oxide nanoparticles also have ample opportunities to bind with the = S group present in 2,5-Dimercapto-1,3,4-thiadiazole. 
 Fig. 8 ( a ) Scheme for Coating of 2, 5- Dimercapto- 1,3,4- thiadiazole on microtiter plates, ( b ) Scheme for coating CDs prepared from Neem and Rosemary. 
 Coating of cds on microtiter plates We measured the concentrations of the Rosemary and Neem extracts CDs. For the first concentration C1, we took 5 mL of the originally prepared CDs aqueous solution, and for the second concentration C2, we mixed 5 mL of DW water in 5 mL of original aqueous solution. For the third concentration C3, we took 5 mL of aqueous solution CDs and mixed 10 ml of distilled water in that. Thus C1, C2 and C3 have a ratio of3:1.5:1.The coating scheme is illustrated in Fig.  8 . Since we used alcohol at later stages of the experiment (e.g., in our case, after 24 h of incubation and growth of bio-films) to check the inhibition of NPs and CDs using photometric method, we can say that the morphologies and surface properties of nanoparticles coated on microtiter plates were least affected by ethanol 12 , 43 , 44 . Fig.  8  (b) shows the coating scheme of CDs prepared using Rosemary and Neem extracts. Biofilm production by crystal Violet biofilm assay The 96-well microtiter plate made of polystyrene was used to detect biofilms. All the plates were kept at 60 °C for 4 h before being used for the We first sterilized the 96-well microtiter plate and placed it in an oven at 60 °C for 4 h and then used it for the biofilm growth process. Three isolates of Candida albicans recovered from clinical samples were used after approval from the Institute’s Human Ethical Clearance Committee. To determine the impact on biofilm production, a crystal violet assay was conducted on microtiter plates with and without nanoparticles. The Crystal violet assay was performed as described previously by Marack et al. 45 . Briefly, fresh Candida albicans was inoculated in a tube containing two millilitres of brain heart infusion broth (BHIB), and the tubes were incubated at 37 °C. After overnight incubation, the broth cultures were diluted 1:20 using fresh BHIB. Microtiter plates were filled with 200 µl of diluted BHIB and incubated at 37 °C for 24 h. The microtiter plates were then emptied, washed thrice with distilled water, and inverted. Next, 200 µl of 1% crystal violet was added to each well and incubated for 15 min. Excess crystal violet was further removed after three washes with distilled water, and the dye was eluted by adding 200 µl of an 80:20 w/v ethanol: acetone mixture in each well and was read at 450 nm using an ELISA reader, and Optical Density (D) was recorded for each well. The optical density is defined as the log of the ratio of the intensities of the incident and transmitted light. 
